\title[The splitting-type channel of a semiprime]{The Splitting-Type Channel of a
Semiprime in a Cyclic Extension}
\subjclass[2020]{11R45, 11N36, 94A17, 05A15}
\keywords{cyclic extension, Frobenius element, splitting type, Euler totient,
Shannon entropy, mutual information, semiprime, symmetrization defect}

\author{Aristotle}
\date{1 September 2026}

\begin{abstract}
Let $K/\Q$ be a cyclic extension of degree $n$, with $\Gal(K/\Q)\cong\Z/n$, and
let $N=pq$ be a semiprime whose two prime factors are unramified in $K$. Each
factor carries a \emph{splitting type} --- the order of its Frobenius element,
equivalently its residue degree in $K$ --- and the Frobenius of $N$ is the
product of the two Frobenii. Under the equidistributed model we determine
exactly the information-theoretic relationship between the residue class of $N$
and the unordered pair of splitting types of its factors.

We prove three results valid for every cyclic order $n$. First, an
\emph{exact enumeration law}: for divisors $d\le e$ of $n$ the number of
Frobenius exponent pairs realising the unordered type pair $\{d,e\}$ is
$\varphi(d)\varphi(e)$ when $d=e$ and $2\varphi(d)\varphi(e)$ otherwise; these
are the only pairs realised, and the counts partition the $n^2$ exponent pairs,
yielding a closed form for the type-pair entropy $H(\Pi)$. Second, a
\emph{which-factor wall}: on the sub-population where the two factors have
distinct splitting types, the Boolean ``which factor carries the smaller type?''
has entropy exactly one bit, while \emph{every} read-out symmetric in the two
factors has mutual information exactly zero with it; the bound is sharp, since
the ordered type read-out recovers the entire bit. Third, a
\emph{symmetrization-defect law}: $H(\Pi)=2H(T)-\#\asym(n)/n^{2}$ with
$\#\asym(n)=n^{2}-\sum_{d\mid n}\varphi(d)^{2}$, so that the entropy cost of
forgetting the order of the two factors equals exactly the probability that the
order is a meaningful question --- the population on which the wall operates.
This gives the sandwich $2H(T)-1\le H(\Pi)<2H(T)$.

At $n=12$ (the case $K=\Q(\zeta_{13})$) these yield
$H(\Pi)=\tfrac78+2\log_2 3$ and $\Ipair(12)=\tfrac5{36}+\log_2 3\approx 1.7239$
bits, with $\#\asym(12)=114$ and defect $19/24$. We further determine the
coarser split-count channel exactly,
$\Isplit(12)=\tfrac{199}{72}+\log_2 3+\tfrac{55}{72}\log_2 5-\tfrac{253}{144}\log_2 11
\approx 0.0445$ bits, prove $0<\Isplit(12)<1/8$ and $\Isplit(12)<\Ipair(12)/10$,
and explain the smallness by a rigidity phenomenon: eleven of the twelve residue
classes of $N$ carry the identical split-count profile $(2,10)$, the twelfth
carrying $(1,11)$.
\end{abstract}

